			<title>In Vogue with Madonna</title>
			<link>http://www.drownedmadonna.com/index/item/5-in-vogue-with-madonna.html</link>
			<guid>http://www.drownedmadonna.com/index/item/5-in-vogue-with-madonna.html</guid>
			<description><![CDATA[<div class="K2FeedIntroText"><p><a href="http://mcv.gaynewsnetwork.com.au" target="_blank" title="gay news network">mcv.gaynewsnetwork.com.au</a></p>
<p>“I was infected with Madonna at an early age,” says <strong>Michael Griffiths </strong>with a laugh. “She came along at almost exactly the right time for me. I was in primary school when she started to make it big, and I don’t think Vale Park Primary School has ever recovered from my performances of 'Holiday' at recess time! As I got older I made it my business to know everything about her, and when it was announced that The Girlie Show was coming to Adelaide I knew I’d get there somehow. I was one of the 40,000 at Adelaide Oval and I’ll never forget it.<br /><br />“My partner and I have decided that we’re going to go and see her next tour, somewhere in the world, and I wish they’d get on and announce the dates!”</p>
<p> </p>
<p><img src="http://www.mcv.gaynewsnetwork.com.au/multisites/mcv/images/stories/540/540-p11.jpg" border="0" width="800" style="border: 0;" /></p>
</div><div class="K2FeedFullText">
<p>Since he moved to Sydney, Griffiths has seen little of his home town. “I studied at the old Flinders Street School of Music and at the Elder Conservatorium, but it was clear that if I wanted to make a career in music theatre I’d have to move," he explains. "So I went first to WAAPA and when I finished there it was off to Sydney – you need to be there and ready to jump at the opportunities when they come up.<br /><br />"But I’ve had a ball so far on all sorts of great shows like Shout! and We Will Rock You and Priscilla, and I’ve been with Jersey Boys for a while now. But I’ve always done a bit of cabaret on the side and it seemed like a good time to do a full solo show together. So with Dean Bryant we’ve put In Vogue together and we’ll be premiering it in Adelaide.”<br /><br />The idea behind In Vogue is to present Madonna’s music, but not to make it a parody. “Look, I love Madonna impersonators, don’t get me wrong,” Griffiths admits. “I mean, some of my most formative Madonna experiences were drag shows at the Mars Bar when I was underage!”<br /><br />But now it’s the real thing. “I want to say a number of things in this show,” Griffiths explains. “Sure, Madonna’s had some amazing hits, but a lot of people don’t know much about her less well-known music. And a huge number of people are completely unaware of how much of her material she writes herself – she’s an incredible composer, one of the most successful songwriters of our time or any other time.” <br /><br />The greatest challenge has been arranging it to suit himself. “Like I said, it’s not an impression. I come out in a tux, and I sing in my own voice – well, mostly! – and that has dictated some of the songs and the arrangements, which I’m furiously completing at the moment.<br /><br />“I think people forget how essential the print media was in those days – we’re talking late 80s, early 90s, when there wasn’t an internet. One of my most treasured possessions was a photo of me and some friends at the Ed that got published in Adelaide GT. We were so excited to see ourselves!” <br /><br /><strong><em>Michael Griffiths, In Vogue: Songs by Madonna</em>, The Artspace, Festival Centre, Adelaide, June 15-17, 2011. Book at BASS.</strong></p></div><div class="K2FeedTags"><ul><li>Michael Griffiths</li><li>Vogue</li><ul></div>]]></description>
